  "summary": "Absolute yeast proteomes and an AlphaFold-based human Golgi interaction map both quantify how proteins are distributed across subcellular compartments.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is quantitative mapping of protein composition within membrane-bound compartments. GolgiFold provides a structure-based protein-protein interaction map of human Golgi complexes, integrating AlphaFold predictions with crosslinking and blue-native proteomics. The yeast study compiles 300 absolute proteomes and applies machine learning and formula fitting to link subcellular protein allocation to growth rate and physiology. Auto-AVS addresses the imaging side of the same problem: an open-source stereology framework that simulates 3D autophagic vacuoles and virtual TEM sections in yeast to test how well size and number are inferred from sections. The new elements are absolute quantitation across 300 yeast proteomes and a structure-based interaction map of Golgi protein complexes.",
